<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Elphinstone, 1st Viscount Keith</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1746">1746</a>–<a href="/years/1823">1823</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Lord Keith, Admiral Keith</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Elphinstone, later Viscount Keith, was a British admiral of the Revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. In 1795 he commanded the fleet that, with troops under James Craig, took the Cape from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and in 1796 he captured a Dutch squadron in Saldanha Bay. He later commanded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the Channel and oversaw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s transfer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">St Helena</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-elphinstone-1st-viscount-keith.jpg" alt="George Elphinstone, 1st Viscount Keith" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Hoppner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vice-admiral_Lord_Keith,_Commander_in_Chief,_Mediterranean,_c_1799_-_John_Hoppner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
